<h3>Introduction</h3>

<p>The convention of using <b>DO</b>, <b>CONSIDER</b>, <b>AVOID</b>, and <b>DO
NOT</b> was introduced in <i>Framework Design Guidelines: Conventions, Idioms,
and Patterns for Reusable .NET Libraries</i> by Krzysztof Cwalina and Brad
Abrams.  This convention has been quite useful beyond the design of .Net
libraries and therefore this set of macros - <b>do</b>, <b>consider</b>,
<b>avoid</b>, <b>do-not</b> and <b>lp-guideline</b> were created to provide
users an easy and consistent way for creating guidelines consistent with the
convention.</p>

<h3>Glyphs - not images!</h3> 

<p>The &quot;&#10004;&quot; and &quot;&#10008;&quot; are glyphs included with
the standard font set.  They can be accessed in html using <b>&amp;#10004;
(&#10004;)</b> and <b>&amp;#10008; (&#10008;)</b>.  As a side note, there are
lighter versions of the glyphs at <b>&amp;#10003; (&#10003;)</b> and
<b>&amp;#10007; (&#10007;)</b>.  The nice thing about using the font glyphs
rather images is that they can be easily stylized with css and export nicely to
word documents. (The glyphs do not seem to carry into the pdf created by Conflunece.)</p>  

<h3>How to use the guidelines</h3>

<p>The following is excerpted from <i>Framework Design Guidelines: Conventions,
Idioms and Patterns for Reusable .NET Libraries</i>.</p>

<p>The guidelines are organized as simple recommendations using <b>Do</b>,
<b>Consider</b>, <b>Avoid</b>, and <b>Do not</b>.  Each guideline describes
either a good or bad practice, and all have a consistent presentation.  Good
practices have a &#10004; in front of them, and bad practices have an &#10008;
in front of them.  The wording of each guideline also indicates how strong the
recommendation is.  For example, a <b>Do</b> guideline is one that should
always be followed.  On the other hand, <b>Consider</b> guidelines should
generally be followed, but if you fully understand the reasoning behind a
guideline and have a good reason to not follow it anyway, you should not feel
bad about breaking the rules. Similarly, <b>Do not</b> guidelines indicate
somethign you should almost never do.  Less strong, <b>Avoid</b> guidelines
indicate that something is generally not a good idea, but there are known cases
where breaking the rule makes sense.</p>

<p>In short...</p>
<table class="confluenceTable">
   <tr><th class="highlight-grey confluenceTh" data-highlight-colour="grey" style="width:100px;">&#10004 <b>DO</b></th><td class="confluenceTd">Emphasizes that this action <b>must always be followed</b> (exceptions are rare).</td></tr>
   <tr><th class="highlight-grey confluenceTh" data-highlight-colour="grey" style="width:100px;">&#10004 <b>CONSIDER</b></th><td class="confluenceTd">Emphasizes that this action <b>should be attempted</b> wherever possible and appropriate (exceptions may occur).</td></tr>
   <tr><th class="highlight-grey confluenceTh" data-highlight-colour="grey" style="width:100px;">&#10008 <b>AVOID</b></th><td class="confluenceTd">Emphasizes that this action <b>should be prevented</b> wherever possible and appropriate (exceptions may occur).</td></tr>
   <tr><th class="highlight-grey confluenceTh" data-highlight-colour="grey" style="width:100px;">&#10008 <b>DO NOT</b></th><td class="confluenceTd">Emphasizes that this action <b>must never happen</b> (exceptions are rare).</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Macro Variations</h3>

<p>There are six guideline macros. The first, <strong>Guideline (Inline)</strong> is the only inline 
macro of the group.  It simply inserts the graphic and text where the macro was placed.  The others
are block macros and will place the graphic the the left of the text much like an indented list.
If there are multiple paragraphs, the left edge of the text for each paragraph will be aligned and
the graphic will be yet further left.  This is to help make it clear that all of text is describing
a single guildline.</p>

<h4>Guideline (Inline)</h4>

<p>The <strong>Guideline (Inline)</strong> inserts the proper graphic and text for one of the four guidelines.</p>

<h4>LP Guideline</h4>

<p>The <strong>LP Guideline</strong> macro is intended to be used by the learning products community. As with the
<strong>Guideline (Inline)</strong> macro, it can represent any of the guidelines via a macro parameter.</p>

<h4>Do, Consider, Avoid and Do Not</h4>

<p>The four macros, <strong>Do</strong>, <strong>Consider</strong>, <strong>Avoid</strong> and <strong>Do Not</strong>,
are all block macros and represent one type of guideline each.</p>
<strong>Guideline (Inline)</strong> macro, it can represent any of the guidelines via a macro parameter.</p>


<h3>Using the macros</h3>

<p>For general usage, the recommendation is to either use the <strong>Guideline (Inline)</strong>
or the block macro that fits your guideline: <b>do</b>, <b>consider</b>, <b>avoid</b> and <b>do-not</b>.
For the block macros, place all of the guideline text (including any images) inside of the macro body.</p>

<p>For LP usage, the recommendation is to use the <b>lp-guideline</b> macro.
This is a single macro that has a parameter to select the appropriate guidance.
Under the hood, <b>{do}</b> and <b>{lp-guideline}</b> with "Do" as the selected
guidance will call on the same code and produce the exact same output.  The
reason for the <b>lp-guideline</b> macro is inform technical writers that the
macro is compatible with the Confluence->Flare->PDF process and therefore may
be used for generating product documentation destined for external customer
use. Note, the LP variant of the macro was done as a single macro with a parameter
to keep the list of LP-* macros short.</p> 

<p>With the block macros, if the guidance term starts the body text, it will be 
automatically removed and the formatted version put in it's place.  For example, if the
end result is &quot;Do call your mother&quot;, the author should insert the <b>Do</b> macro
and can either put &quot;call your mother&quot; into the body of the macro or
&quot;Do call your mother&quot;.  Both will generate the same output.  The author
of the macro suggests inserting the guidance term as it makes it easier to read
in the editor.</p>

<h3><strong>Auto</strong> Guidance</h3>

<p>In the <strong>Guideline (Inline)</strong>, and <strong>LP Guideline</strong> macros,
there is an option to set the guidance to <strong>Auto</strong>. If set, the macro will 
use the first word(s) in the body of the macro to make this determination.  For example,
if the body of the macro starts with &quot;Do pay your bills.&quot; the macro will automatically
set the rendering mode to <strong>Do</strong>.</p>

<h3>Parameters</h3>

<p><strong>Guidance</strong>: This value determines what guidance icon and term to insert.</p>
